const porphyrySections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence', 'A major philosopher recovered from an uneven corpus', [
    'Porphyry of Tyre wrote across logic, metaphysics, psychology, ethics, literary interpretation, mathematics, religious practice, and controversy. Yet this impressive range is known through a sharply uneven archive. The Life of Plotinus, Isagoge, Sentences, On Abstinence, Letter to Marcella, and several commentaries survive substantially; many theoretical and polemical works survive only in excerpts, hostile reports, or titles. Even an influential anonymous Parmenides commentary has been attributed to him and then forcefully disputed. Any portrait built from the surviving practical and introductory texts may therefore underrepresent the ambitions of his lost philosophy.',
    'That imbalance changes the right historical question. Porphyry should neither be reduced to Plotinus’s editor nor promoted into a fully recoverable architect of one seamless system. Surviving works address distinct audiences and tasks, and chronology is often insecure. Later Platonists frequently pair Plotinus and Porphyry, but their testimony can compress disagreement and development. This article consequently distinguishes direct texts, Porphyry’s retrospective reports, later reception, and modern reconstruction. Where a lost work is involved—especially Against the Christians or commentaries known through fragments—the loss is part of the claim rather than a footnote to it.',
  ], ['neoplatonism', 'platonism'], ['plotinus', 'iamblichus'], ['Life of Plotinus', 'Isagoge', 'Sentences']),
  section('life-circle', 'Tyre, Athens, Rome, and the Plotinian circle', [
    'The conventional dates about 234 to about 305 CE are estimates. Porphyry identifies Tyre as his origin and records that Longinus renamed him, while later sources add material of varying reliability. He studied with Longinus at Athens before joining Plotinus in Rome in 263. The Life describes an active seminar culture in which texts were read, objections invited, and positions tested. Porphyry initially resisted Plotinus on intelligible being, then wrote a retraction after Amelius answered him. The episode depicts apprenticeship as argument, although its form also serves the biography’s picture of Plotinus’s intellectual authority.',
    'Porphyry reports that a severe crisis led Plotinus to detect his intention of ending his life and advise travel to Sicily. He was away when Plotinus died in 270. Details after this point are sparse: the location of his later teaching, the extent of a Roman school, and a direct teacher-pupil relationship with Iamblichus are less secure than tidy genealogies imply. His late marriage to Marcella is reflected in an extant exhortatory letter. Around 301 he completed the edition of Plotinus that frames most subsequent access to his teacher. This is a mobile scholarly life, not a securely documented institutional succession.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'neoplatonism'], ['plotinus', 'iamblichus'], ['Life of Plotinus', 'Letter to Marcella']),
  section('edition', 'The Life of Plotinus as biography and editorial argument', [
    'The Life is invaluable eyewitness testimony, but it is not a neutral dossier. It presents Plotinus as an exemplary philosopher whose bodily reserve, generosity, teaching, contemplative achievement, and final illness disclose a way of life. Porphyry identifies participants and controversies in the Roman circle, names texts used in seminars, and places his own mistakes and correction inside the narrative. These details let historians reconstruct a community. The same choices also guide admiration: the pupil who orders the master’s writings simultaneously orders the reader’s image of the master.',
    'Chapters 24–26 explain the famous arrangement of fifty-four treatises into six groups of nine. Porphyry also supplies a chronological list, so he did not simply mistake his thematic sequence for order of composition. The Enneadic design moves from ethical and embodied questions toward soul, intellect, and first principles, but individual placements can be editorially strategic rather than a transcript of Plotinus’s development. Titles, divisions, and neighbors affect interpretation before an argument begins. Porphyry’s edition therefore preserved extraordinary material while becoming a philosophical intervention that later readers must learn to see.',
  ], ['neoplatonism', 'epistemology'], ['plotinus'], ['Life of Plotinus', 'Enneads']),
  section('logic', 'The Isagoge: elementary distinctions with a long afterlife', [
    'The Isagoge introduces five predicables needed for study of Aristotle’s Categories: genus, species, difference, property, and accident. Porphyry shows how answers to “what is it?” and related questions organize classification and predication. The work is pedagogically modest, not a miniature statement of all his metaphysics. Its celebrated opening postpones questions about whether genera and species subsist, whether they are bodily or incorporeal, and whether they exist separately or in sensible particulars. The postponement marks those issues as advanced; it does not reveal a secret preference for medieval realism or nominalism.',
    'Greek, Syriac, Arabic, and Latin readers made this small introduction a durable gateway to logic. Boethius’s translations and commentaries gave its opening questions a decisive place in Latin disputes about universals, but those disputes cannot simply be assigned back to Porphyry in their later form. His logical work is better understood as disciplined preparation: students learn to distinguish modes of predication before taking on metaphysics. Porphyry also commented directly on the Categories and sought ways for Aristotelian logical analysis to operate within Platonic philosophy. Harmony here is a program of interpretation, not proof that Plato and Aristotle say the same thing everywhere.',
  ], ['logic', 'aristotelianism', 'medieval-scholasticism'], ['aristotle', 'boethius'], ['Isagoge', 'Categories']),
  section('soul-practice', 'Soul, virtue, and philosophy as practiced separation', [
    'The Sentences condense themes familiar from Plotinus while often using a distinct vocabulary. Soul is incorporeal and not located in body as one extended thing inside another; its relation to embodiment involves activity and attention. Ethical formation aims to turn the person away from domination by passions and toward intelligible life. Yet the surviving evidence does not license a complete map of Porphyry’s psychology. Works on soul are fragmentary, To Gaurus has a complicated attribution history, and later Christian or Platonist witnesses select the claims useful to their own arguments.',
    'Porphyry’s practical writings make philosophy a regimen rather than an inventory of propositions. The Letter to Marcella recommends habits of judgment, self-command, reverence, and inward orientation. Different grades of virtue organize life from civic moderation through purification toward contemplation. “Separation” from body should therefore be read as a reordering of agency, not the physical removal of a soul from all responsibility. The aspiration remains demanding and socially selective: the leisure and education assumed by the philosophical life invite questions about whose circumstances permit such disciplines and how care for others fits an ideal of inward return.',
  ], ['ethics', 'philosophy-of-mind', 'neoplatonism'], ['plotinus'], ['Sentences', 'Letter to Marcella']),
  section('abstinence', 'Animals, sacrifice, and the discipline of appetite', [
    'On Abstinence addresses Firmus Castricius, a philosopher who had resumed eating meat. Across four books Porphyry collects ethical, physiological, religious, and ethnographic arguments for abstention. He links animal killing with appetite, disturbance, and customary sacrifice, while also asking whether animals possess reason and whether justice can extend to them. The work is cumulative and sometimes preserves arguments or reports without making every premise equally Porphyry’s own. It also explicitly focuses on people pursuing the contemplative life, so its prescription should not automatically be treated as a universal civic diet.',
    'The treatise complicates the convenient contrast between rational philosophy and “pagan ritual.” Porphyry criticizes blood sacrifice while defending reverence for gods and forms of offering he regards as purer. Diet trains attention, limits luxury, and embodies a theory about kinship among living beings. Some ethnographic examples depend on idealized descriptions of distant peoples or priestly groups and require historical caution. Even so, the argument is philosophically serious: daily consumption exposes whether a person’s account of justice and purification reaches beyond speech. Its tensions—ascetic elitism, animal rationality, inherited cult, and moral consistency—are integral to the work.',
  ], ['ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], [], ['On Abstinence']),
  section('religion', 'Oracles, ritual limits, and disagreement with Iamblichus', [
    'Porphyry was neither a modern secular rationalist nor simply an advocate of every traditional rite. In Philosophy from Oracles, known only in fragments, he assembled and interpreted revelatory material; elsewhere he investigated images, myths, sacrifice, and demons. At the same time, evidence suggests that he limited what ritual or magic could accomplish, reserving the highest ascent for intellectual and ethical purification. Since the relevant works are incomplete and their chronology uncertain, scholars cannot chart a confident evolution from credulity to rationalism or back again. Different genres and audiences may explain some apparent variation.',
    'Iamblichus’s Reply to Porphyry answers questions from Porphyry’s lost Letter to Anebo. It has become tempting to stage their disagreement as reason against superstition. The surviving relation is subtler and asymmetrical: Porphyry’s questions are partly reconstructed through an opponent, while Iamblichus contests both religious judgments and assumptions about soul and divine causation. Porphyry’s position is therefore not fully recoverable from the reply. What can be said is that his inquiries made ritual efficacy a philosophical problem and helped provoke a later Platonism in which theurgy received a more systematic defense.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'neoplatonism'], ['iamblichus'], ['Philosophy from Oracles', 'Letter to Anebo', 'Reply to Porphyry']),
  section('polemics', 'Against the Christians and the hazards of hostile preservation', [
    'Against the Christians was a large polemic, traditionally counted as fifteen books, but no continuous text survives. Christian authors quote, paraphrase, and answer material attributed to Porphyry; imperial orders later targeted copies for destruction. Reconstructing the work from these remains is difficult because opponents may excerpt strategically, several anti-Christian arguments circulated under his name, and the original structure is debated. Claims that Porphyry anticipated modern biblical criticism should thus be narrowed to identifiable ancient arguments about authorship, prophecy, chronology, inconsistency, or interpretation rather than a recovered critical method as a whole.',
    'Porphyry’s objections emerged from a committed Hellenic philosophical and religious identity. He assessed Christian scripture and communal claims against standards formed by Platonic metaphysics, philology, moral exemplarity, and older sacred traditions. That setting explains both analytical force and polemical boundary-making. It also prevents the false image of a neutral critic standing outside religion. Later Christian writers sometimes respected his learning while treating him as a dangerous adversary, and their responses shaped what survived. Every claim about this work must therefore name the mediating witness or rely on a modern fragment study that makes the reconstruction explicit.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'epistemology'], ['origen', 'augustine'], ['Against the Christians']),
  section('interpretation', 'Allegory, commentary, and authority', [
    'On the Cave of the Nymphs interprets a brief Homeric scene through cosmology, soul, cult, and symbol. Its method exemplifies Porphyry’s broader conviction that an authoritative text can carry meanings not exhausted by surface narrative. Commentary asks about a work’s purpose, vocabulary, sequence, and compatibility with philosophical truth. Such reading can retrieve layers of ritual and intellectual tradition that a flat literalism misses. It can also insulate inherited authority by assigning coherence that the source does not demonstrably possess. The philosophical value lies partly in making that risk visible.',
    'The same issue governs Porphyry’s work on Plato and Aristotle. He helped make Aristotelian logic preparatory to Platonic study and sought agreements across texts without abolishing every difference. Much of this commentary is lost, so later reports cannot support a detailed syllabus with complete confidence. Still, his method changed the institutional life of philosophy: interpretation, textual organization, and staged difficulty became central forms of reasoning. The commentator does not merely repeat a canonical author; choices about a passage’s aim and pedagogical level decide which apparent contradictions need resolution and which questions a student is ready to ask.',
  ], ['aesthetics', 'logic', 'platonism'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['On the Cave of the Nymphs', 'Categories']),
  section('legacy-reading', 'A mediator whose losses demand disciplined reading', [
    'Porphyry’s reception crosses boundaries that later surveys often separate. His edition governs access to Plotinus. The Isagoge and Categories work entered Byzantine, Syriac, Arabic, and Latin logical traditions, with Boethius especially important in the West. His psychology and exegesis informed later Platonists and Christian authors, even when they opposed his religious commitments. Iamblichus developed positions partly through criticism of him. Because different works traveled through different languages and institutions, there is no single “Porphyrian legacy”; edition, logic, ascetic practice, allegory, and polemic generated distinct histories.',
    'A sound reading path begins with the Life of Plotinus while separating eyewitness report from encomiastic design, then uses the Isagoge to observe Porphyry’s elementary precision. On Abstinence reveals philosophical practice and religious argument in daily life. Only afterward should fragments of Philosophy from Oracles or Against the Christians be approached with their editors’ cautions visible. The central lesson is methodological as well as doctrinal: transmission can be an original philosophical act, but a successful mediator may become harder to see behind the authors and traditions he made readable.',
    'Comparison across these works should preserve their different voices. The Isagoge’s restraint, the Life’s personal recollection, the Sentences’ compression, and On Abstinence’s cumulative advocacy do not automatically combine into one developmental sequence. Where they converge, they show a thinker concerned with the conditions under which learners become capable of truth: reliable texts, clear distinctions, corrected desire, and appropriate forms of reverence. Where they differ, the first explanation should be genre and incomplete survival rather than a dramatic conversion invented to make the evidence tell a simple story.',
    'Porphyry also offers a useful warning about intellectual originality. His most visible achievements often serve another author or an inherited curriculum, yet selection and organization create new philosophical possibilities. The later problem of universals was not his finished doctrine, and the Enneads are not his own treatises, but both reception histories were made possible by decisions he took. Responsible study can recognize that agency without crediting him with every later consequence. It can also admit what the lost theoretical works might change without filling the absence through confident speculation. In that respect, uncertainty is not an editorial failure: it is the most accurate account of a thinker whose historical effectiveness greatly exceeds the portion of his writing that survives.',
  ], ['neoplatonism', 'logic', 'medieval-scholasticism'], ['plotinus', 'iamblichus', 'boethius'], ['Life of Plotinus', 'Isagoge', 'On Abstinence']),
];

