const iamblichusSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence', 'Iamblichus without a single surviving system', [
    'Iamblichus is central to later Platonism, but the evidence is not a continuous philosophical system in his own hand. The Reply to Porphyry survives complete under the later title On the Mysteries, and four works from a larger Pythagorean project remain. His influential commentaries on Plato and Aristotle are lost, as are most theological writings. Psychology is reconstructed from excerpts, especially those preserved by Stobaeus, while later commentators quote or adapt his exegesis. The surviving pattern favors ritual defense and Pythagorean pedagogy over the commentaries through which his students probably learned much of his philosophy.',
    'This archive makes two shortcuts unsafe. First, the complete Reply should not be treated as a substitute for everything Iamblichus believed. Second, Proclus and other later Platonists cannot simply fill every gap, because they developed inherited distinctions for their own settings. Iamblichus did reshape metaphysics, curriculum, psychology, and religious practice, but reconstruction must label its routes: direct treatise, excerpt, later quotation, or scholarly inference. “Neoplatonism” itself is a modern grouping of related schools rather than the ancient name of one institution with a uniform doctrine from Plotinus onward.',
  ], ['neoplatonism', 'platonism'], ['plotinus', 'porphyry', 'proclus'], ['Reply to Porphyry', 'On the Pythagorean Way of Life']),
  section('life-school', 'A Syrian teacher and a discontinuous genealogy', [
    'The usual lifespan, about 242 to about 325 CE, is approximate. Eunapius’s later biography is openly admiring, and even Iamblichus’s birthplace has been disputed between two places called Chalcis. Evidence connects him to an influential Syrian family, to study with Anatolius and probably Porphyry, and eventually to a teaching community at Apamea. A break with Porphyry is plausible but cannot be dated securely. Stories of wonder-working and divine favor illuminate his later reputation more safely than they document ordinary events in his career.',
    'His school had identifiable students, including Sopater, Aedesius, and Dexippus, yet it was not an institutional continuation of Plotinus’s Roman circle. Later narratives of a golden chain make philosophical inheritance appear seamless; geography and evidence show separate communities connected by texts, teachers, and retrospective claims. This matters because “Iamblichean” influence did not require every later Platonist to repeat a fixed creed. He supplied exegetical rules, metaphysical distinctions, a curriculum, and an account of theurgy that successors selected and reorganized. School formation here is a historical process, not a single founding moment.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'neoplatonism'], ['porphyry', 'proclus'], []),
  section('abamon', 'Abamon answers a letter we no longer possess', [
    'The work conventionally called On the Mysteries presents itself more precisely as the reply of the Egyptian prophet Abamon to Porphyry’s Letter to the priest Anebo. Porphyry’s letter survives only in fragments and through the reply’s framing, so the exchange is unequal. Iamblichus writes behind an assumed Egyptian persona and sometimes corrects what he portrays as Porphyry’s Greek habits of classification. The persona is not a trivial mask: it places authority, cultural translation, and the limits of philosophical questioning inside the argument. It also means that “Abamon says” and “Iamblichus argues through Abamon” are sometimes safer formulations than direct autobiography.',
    'The ten-book reply treats gods, angels, demons, heroes, divination, prayer, sacrifice, astrology, and the route to happiness. Its organization follows Porphyry’s difficulties rather than presenting a neutral textbook. The traditional Latin-derived title encourages readers to exoticize the work as Egyptian mystery, while the original framing emphasizes a polemical solution. Modern translations use the standard critical text and conventional book-chapter divisions, which make claims inspectable. What remains missing is equally important: the full wording and order of Porphyry’s challenge, and an independent account of how contemporary practitioners received Iamblichus’s defense.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'neoplatonism'], ['porphyry'], ['Reply to Porphyry', 'Letter to Anebo']),
  section('divine-causation', 'Why knowledge does not initiate contact with the gods', [
    'Abamon’s opening move rejects the idea that human assent establishes the gods. Knowledge of them is said to be preceded by a relation grounded in divine presence. This does not make inquiry worthless; it relocates agency. If the gods are genuinely superior causes, their activity cannot wait upon a correct mental representation produced by an embodied knower. Iamblichus accordingly criticizes questions that measure divine reality by human passions, bodies, or local movement. Different classes of superior beings require different accounts rather than one anthropomorphic model.',
    'The metaphysical point supports the ritual one. A return generated entirely by our reasoning would make the higher dependent on the lower and salvation an achievement of intellectual self-elevation. Iamblichus instead makes divine causation prior while preserving human preparation and discrimination. This position is sometimes called pessimistic because it stresses how fully the individual soul descends into embodied life. Yet the same diagnosis allows sensible things to become sites of divine activity. Dependence is not absence: the world can bear symbols and powers precisely because higher causes are not confined to intellect as humans exercise it.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['plotinus', 'porphyry'], ['Reply to Porphyry']),
  section('theurgy', 'Theurgy as divinely grounded ritual action', [
    'Theurgy names sacred action whose efficacy originates with the gods. In the Reply it includes prayer, sacrifice, invocations, material symbols, and other rites, but no single procedure captures the whole. Symbols do not coerce gods as though a technician possessed a superior mechanism. Their power depends on divine causes that have placed affinities throughout the cosmos. Correct ritual joins participants to those causal series in ways discursive thought alone cannot manufacture. Fraud, confusion, and lower spiritual agencies remain possible, so Iamblichus devotes substantial attention to classification and signs rather than granting every extraordinary event divine status.',
    'Calling this “magic” can obscure the argument by importing a modern opposition between irrational ritual and rational philosophy. Iamblichus offers philosophical reasons for the limits of philosophy understood as autonomous cognition; theurgy completes a whole life of ethical, mathematical, exegetical, and religious formation. Conversely, sympathetic language should not erase historical strangeness or protect claims of ritual efficacy from scrutiny. The central philosophical issue is whether embodied agents can be transformed through material and communal practices whose meaning exceeds what they consciously represent, and how genuine mediation could be distinguished from projection or manipulation.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'metaphysics'], ['porphyry'], ['Reply to Porphyry']),
  section('soul', 'Embodiment, soul, and the limits of Plotinian ascent', [
    'Fragments of On the Soul and reports in later authors suggest that Iamblichus rejected the view that the soul’s highest part remains undescended. The human soul changes through embodiment in a way that distinguishes it sharply from intellect and from higher souls. This helps explain why inward intellectual recognition cannot by itself restore the whole person. The evidence, however, comes through excerpts selected by Stobaeus and later debates; a fully ordered Iamblichean psychology cannot be read straight from one preserved treatise. Claims about exact levels and vehicles of soul must remain tied to fragment editions.',
    'Iamblichus also differentiates kinds of soul and their activities, refusing to make every instance of life or rationality operate identically. Human discursive thought unfolds through time and can be divided, distracted, and educated. Ethical virtue and contemplation remain necessary because ritual is not a shortcut around character or understanding. Theurgy answers a metaphysical limit, not laziness. This conjunction changes later Platonism: embodied vulnerability, cosmic mediation, and divine initiative become more pronounced, while the philosophical curriculum explains how a student acquires the discernment needed to participate responsibly in practices that surpass discursive knowledge.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-mind', 'neoplatonism'], ['plotinus', 'porphyry'], ['On the Soul']),
  section('pythagorean-project', 'Pythagoras as a program for philosophical formation', [
    'Four surviving books belong to a larger project usually called On Pythagoreanism: On the Pythagorean Way of Life, Protrepticus, On General Mathematical Science, and an Introduction to Nicomachus’s Arithmetic. The exact ancient title and planned extent are disputed. These books incorporate earlier material extensively. The Protrepticus preserves long passages from lost exhortatory literature, and the Life draws on prior biographies. Their derivative character does not make them useless, but it prevents the portrait of Pythagoras and his community from functioning as straightforward sixth-century BCE evidence.',
    'For Iamblichus, Pythagoras organizes a path joining exemplary life, communal discipline, mathematics, theology, and revelation. Biographical scenes show how habits and relationships prepare intellectual achievement; mathematical study mediates between sensible plurality and intelligible order. The project thereby offers more than antiquarian history. It constructs an authoritative genealogy for late-antique education. Readers must hold together invention and preservation: Iamblichus saves earlier texts that would otherwise be lost, yet selects and arranges them to authorize his own account of philosophical life. The historical Pythagoreans and the Iamblichean Pythagorean program are related but not identical objects.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'ethics', 'metaphysics'], ['pythagoras'], ['On the Pythagorean Way of Life', 'Protrepticus', 'On General Mathematical Science']),
  section('exegesis', 'Commentary, curriculum, and reconstructing lost teaching', [
    'Iamblichus’s commentaries on Plato and Aristotle are lost, but later commentators preserve enough quotations to show major features of his practice. An authoritative dialogue has a single governing aim, or skopos, and its parts must be interpreted in relation to that aim. A structured sequence of Platonic dialogues begins with Alcibiades and advances toward the Timaeus and Parmenides, while Aristotelian study supplies earlier preparation. This curriculum is reconstructed from later prolegomena and quotations, so details should not all be assigned the same level of certainty.',
    'The method treats textual interpretation as spiritual and intellectual formation. Apparent digressions can reveal the dialogue’s governing purpose; characters, dramatic settings, and arguments occupy levels within a coordinated teaching. Such charitable unity can generate powerful readings, but it can also suppress genuine plurality in Plato or harmonize inherited authorities too readily. Iamblichus’s importance lies partly in making the commentator a philosophical architect. His lost works survive as constraints and possibilities inside Proclus, Simplicius, and others, but later systems should not be reverse-engineered into his exact doctrine without explicit evidence.',
  ], ['platonism', 'aristotelianism', 'neoplatonism'], ['plato', 'aristotle', 'proclus'], ['Lost commentaries on Plato and Aristotle']),
  section('metaphysics-mathematics', 'Multiplicity above and mathematics between', [
    'Later evidence credits Iamblichus with multiplying distinctions among first principles, intelligible and intellective realities, divine unities, and lower orders. These distinctions respond to recurring causal problems: how can a transcendent source produce determinate plurality, and how can each level depend on what exceeds it without collapsing into it? Because many technical works are lost, scholars often reconstruct the architecture from fragments and Proclean parallels. The secure conclusion is directional rather than diagrammatic: Iamblichus presses beyond a simple One–Intellect–Soul summary and makes mediation a fundamental explanatory task.',
    'Mathematics has a corresponding intermediate role. On General Mathematical Science treats mathematical objects and methods as educationally transformative, not merely useful calculations. Number, limit, and the unlimited help thought move from sensible examples toward stable structures while retaining plurality. This interest also informs the Pythagorean presentation of philosophy and later influenced Proclus’s work on Euclid. It would be misleading, however, to collapse mathematics into coded theology or to claim that every later Proclean distinction already existed fully in Iamblichus. The surviving texts support a program of mediation more securely than a finished table of entities.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'epistemology'], ['pythagoras', 'proclus'], ['On General Mathematical Science']),
  section('ethics-community', 'Virtue, friendship, and political counsel', [
    'Theurgy does not exhaust Iamblichus’s practical philosophy. Surviving letters discuss friendship, concord, marriage, ruling, and virtues, while the Pythagorean works imagine a disciplined community ordered toward shared goods. Later reports describe grades of virtue extending from natural and civic formation through purification and contemplation to theurgic union. Some details belong to broader later Platonist tradition and are difficult to assign uniquely to Iamblichus. Still, the pattern resists the idea that ritual bypasses ethics: stable character and rightly ordered relations prepare the person to receive rather than counterfeit divine activity.',
    'This ideal also has political risks. Hierarchy can explain differentiated responsibility and service, yet it can sanctify elite authority or treat inherited order as cosmically guaranteed. Sopater’s later involvement at Constantine’s court shows that Iamblichean networks were not sealed away from imperial politics, although it does not yield a complete political doctrine. A careful reading asks how dependence on higher causes affects human accountability. If divine initiative is primary, agents still need practices of justice and discernment; otherwise appeals to sacred order could excuse precisely the disorder they claim to overcome.',
  ], ['ethics', 'political-philosophy'], ['proclus'], ['Letters', 'On the Pythagorean Way of Life']),
  section('reception-reading', 'A plural legacy and a cautious route into it', [
    'Iamblichus influenced later schools in Pergamon, Athens, and Alexandria through students, curricular practices, commentaries, and theological methods. Emperor Julian admired him; Proclus and Damascius developed Iamblichean distinctions; Simplicius preserves parts of his Aristotelian exegesis. This is a broad reception, not proof of doctrinal uniformity. Different communities combined his legacy with Syrianus, the Chaldean Oracles, local cult, and their own interpretive problems. The modern label “later Neoplatonism” is useful only when it leaves these differences visible rather than turning Iamblichus into one stage in an inevitable system.',
    'Begin with selected books of the Reply to Porphyry, always remembering the Abamon persona and the missing letter. Then read the Pythagorean Life as a late-antique educational construction, not transparent archaic biography. Fragments of On the Soul and modern specialist studies clarify why embodiment and divine causation make theurgy philosophically necessary within this framework. The best guiding question is not whether Iamblichus abandoned reason. It is how reasoning, character, inherited text, material symbol, communal rite, and higher agency can belong to one account of transformation—and where that account remains vulnerable to historical and philosophical criticism.',
    'A final discipline is to keep levels of confidence visible. The Reply securely witnesses an argument voiced as Abamon; the Pythagorean books securely witness a late-antique program built from earlier materials; excerpts securely preserve selected psychological discussions. A complete school system inferred from Proclus is less secure. This graduated evidence does not diminish Iamblichus’s importance. It explains why his influence can be unmistakable even when the exact contours of many doctrines remain disputed.',
  ], ['neoplatonism', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['porphyry', 'proclus'], ['Reply to Porphyry', 'On the Pythagorean Way of Life', 'On the Soul']),
];

