const proclusSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence', 'Systematic works inside a larger teaching practice', [
    'Proclus is unusually well represented for a late-antique philosopher. The Elements of Theology, Platonic Theology, major Platonic commentaries, works on providence and evil, mathematical writings, and seven hymns survive, although some texts are incomplete and much else is lost. This abundance creates its own distortion. The geometrical sequence of the Elements can make him appear to have written one closed metaphysical machine, while commentaries show arguments emerging from seminars, inherited interpretations, and the wording of particular Platonic dialogues. Hymns and biography place the same thinker within prayer, festival, and theurgic practice.',
    'A reliable profile therefore resists two reductions: Proclus is neither only a system-builder nor only a religious scholastic. Formal propositions, exegesis, pedagogy, and ritual answer different but connected tasks. His pupil Marinus supplies most biographical detail in an encomium designed to display ascending grades of virtue, not in an administrative record. Syrianus’s influence is so pervasive that the boundary between the teacher’s doctrines and Proclus’s elaborations is often unclear. The evidence is rich enough for precision, but not for treating every part of the corpus as one genre or every innovation as securely individual.',
  ], ['neoplatonism', 'platonism'], ['iamblichus'], ['Elements of Theology', 'Platonic Theology', 'Hymns']),
  section('life-school', 'From Lycia and Alexandria to the Athenian school', [
    'Proclus was born in 412 at Constantinople into a Lycian family and was raised largely at Xanthos. He began studies oriented toward law and rhetoric, then pursued philosophy and mathematics at Alexandria. Around 430 or 431 he moved to Athens, studying first with Plutarch of Athens and then intensively with Syrianus. After Syrianus’s death Proclus became head, or diadochos, of the Platonic school and retained that role for nearly fifty years until his death in 485. The chronology is comparatively firm, but almost all narrative texture comes from admiring students and later school memory.',
    'Marinus depicts a demanding daily schedule of lectures, seminars, writing, prayer, ritual observance, and care for students. “Academy” is convenient but can mislead if it implies direct institutional continuity from Plato’s fourth-century BCE foundation. The fifth-century Athenian school claimed Platonic succession while operating in a different legal, religious, and urban world. It also existed amid Christian imperial dominance and episodes of local tension; Marinus reports that Proclus temporarily left Athens after conflict without supplying a modern political history. School leadership joined teaching, household, textual authority, and pagan religious identity.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'neoplatonism'], ['plato'], ['Proclus, or On Happiness']),
  section('elements-method', 'What the Elements demonstrates—and what it omits', [
    'The Elements of Theology presents 211 propositions, each followed by a proof. Its opening establishes that plurality participates unity, then develops causal priority, participation, procession, return, self-constitution, and orders of intellect and soul. The form resembles mathematical demonstration: later results depend on distinctions secured earlier. It is an extraordinary guide to Proclean causal grammar. Yet it contains few named citations and does not display the scriptural-exegetical labor by which Proclus believed Platonic theology was recovered. A reader should not confuse formal economy with the whole historical practice of his philosophy.',
    'The proofs also require close attention to recurring technical contrasts: cause and participant, unparticipated and participated terms, self-sufficient and dependent beings, universal and particular series. These are not decorative levels added to make a hierarchy impressive. Each tries to preserve both the presence of a cause in effects and the cause’s superiority to them. Whether every distinction succeeds is a further question. The proper critical test asks what explanatory work a new level performs and whether the premises establish it, rather than either admiring the architecture from afar or dismissing it as arbitrary multiplication.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'logic'], [], ['Elements of Theology']),
  section('causation', 'Remaining, procession, and return', [
    'A Proclean effect remains in its cause, proceeds from it, and returns toward it. “Remaining” means that production does not detach an effect from the power that constitutes it. “Procession” preserves real difference and plurality; the effect is not simply the cause under another name. “Return” describes the orientation by which a being seeks its good and completion in its source. Elements propositions 30–39 develop this triadic pattern, while other propositions analyze how causes communicate their characters through ordered series. The pattern applies analogically, not as one temporal event repeated at every level.',
    'Participation answers a related problem. A cause can be present to many effects without being divided into pieces, while effects receive it according to their own capacities. Proclus distinguishes an unparticipated source, participated mediations, and particular participants to explain this distribution. Critics may wonder whether the mediating terms clarify dependence or merely rename it. Proclus’s advantage is that he does not let “emanation” do unexplained work. His vocabulary forces questions about asymmetry, likeness, difference, and the mode of presence. The system’s enduring value often lies in this disciplined analysis even for readers who reject its populated hierarchy.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'neoplatonism'], ['plotinus', 'iamblichus'], ['Elements of Theology']),
  section('one-henads', 'The One, henads, and polytheistic theology', [
    'The first principle is beyond being and intellect because every determinate multiplicity depends upon unity. But a single highest One does not by itself explain the distinct divine causal series recognized in Platonic texts and traditional cult. Proclus therefore develops henads, divine unities that are participated while the absolutely One remains unparticipated. Henads ground distinctive chains of beings and divine properties without becoming ordinary members of the ontological inventory. The technical account is distributed across the Elements, Platonic Theology, and commentaries, so no one slogan captures their relation.',
    'Platonic Theology argues that Plato’s dialogues disclose an ordered theology when read with appropriate exegetical principles. Names and myths from Homer, Hesiod, Orphic tradition, and the Chaldean Oracles are coordinated with philosophical distinctions. This is neither a modern comparative history of religions nor a simple allegorical codebook. It is a committed attempt to show harmony among Hellenic revelations under Platonic science. The method can generate striking integrations, yet it also protects canonical authorities through complex reconciliation. Proclus’s polytheism belongs to the substance of his metaphysics, not an optional cultural ornament placed around an otherwise secular system.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'metaphysics'], ['plato', 'iamblichus'], ['Platonic Theology', 'Elements of Theology']),
  section('commentary', 'Plato read in the classroom', [
    'Proclus’s commentaries preserve layers of Platonic interpretation while advancing his own positions. They ask for each dialogue’s skopos, explain its dramatic setting and sequence, report earlier readings, raise objections, and resolve difficulties. The Timaeus commentary treats Plato’s account of nature as a science that reaches the divine and intelligible causes of the cosmos, rather than merely cataloguing physical changes. Such works likely grew from lectures and seminars, so repetition and local argument are clues to pedagogy rather than defects measured against the compressed Elements.',
    'Commentary also limits claims about a freestanding “Proclean system.” The categories through which Proclus thinks are continually tested against Plato’s wording, and different dialogues disclose different levels of reality. He assumes deep harmony and a divinely inspired Plato, commitments modern historical readers need not share. Still, his practice models a demanding form of charitable interpretation: predecessors are named, objections developed, and small textual decisions made consequential. Reading a commentary beside the Elements shows movement in both directions—system guides exegesis, while difficult passages force the system to articulate distinctions that a summary can conceal.',
  ], ['platonism', 'neoplatonism'], ['plato'], ['Commentary on the Timaeus', 'Commentary on the Parmenides']),
  section('soul-learning', 'Soul, divided thought, and the work of education', [
    'Soul occupies an intermediate position. Unlike intellect’s unified cognition, human discursive thinking unfolds through sequence, yet soul contains reason-principles that make knowledge and recollection possible. Embodiment can scatter attention among lower activities without erasing the soul’s intelligible grounding. Proclus differentiates perception, imagination, opinion, discursive reasoning, and intellection; education orders rather than simply suppresses these capacities. His account should not be reduced to a generic body-denying dualism, although ascent and purification do assign higher value to forms of life less governed by bodily passion.',
    'Mathematics is crucial because it trains the soul to recognize stable relations in plurality. Proclus’s Euclid commentary combines philosophy of mathematics, historical reports, and interpretation of demonstration. Mathematical objects occupy an intermediate field suited to discursive reasoning, allowing students to move beyond sense while not pretending to possess undivided intellect. This pedagogical setting explains why formal proof matters beyond style. The learner’s sequence mirrors the metaphysical problem of mediation: knowledge must proceed through differences while recovering the unity that makes relations intelligible. School practice thus belongs inside, not outside, Proclean metaphysics.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-mind', 'epistemology'], ['plato'], ['Commentary on Euclid', 'Commentary on the Timaeus']),
  section('evil-providence', 'Providence, freedom, and evil without an evil principle', [
    'Proclus defends providence while refusing to make evil a positive first principle. In works preserved partly through medieval translations, he argues that evil is parasitic, arising when particular goods and activities fail to achieve their proper order. Matter alone is not an independent evil substance, and what is bad in one respect may remain good insofar as it has being and causal powers. This privative strategy protects the goodness and unity of first causes, but it faces the persistent challenge of explaining the experienced force and patterned recurrence of harm.',
    'Providence operates universally while fate orders embodied conditions; rational souls retain forms of self-motion and responsibility. The distinctions aim to show how divine governance, natural necessity, contingency, and choice can coexist without occupying the same causal level. They do not make tragedy disappear. A strong reading tests whether calling evil partial disorder adequately addresses victims, institutions, and deliberate cruelty. Proclus’s framework is philosophically valuable because it locates the difficulty precisely: a complete causal order must explain defect without either deriving evil straightforwardly from the Good or granting evil an equal metaphysical source.',
  ], ['ethics', 'metaphysics'], [], ['On the Existence of Evils', 'Ten Problems Concerning Providence']),
  section('hymns-theurgy', 'Prayer, hymns, and theurgic unification', [
    'Seven hymns survive under Proclus’s name, addressed to gods including Helios, Aphrodite, Athena, and the Muses. Together with Marinus’s biography, they show that prayer and ritual were ordinary parts of his philosophical life. The hymns ask for purification, illumination, protection, and return through the divine series to which a deity gives unity. They are not merely poetic illustrations of propositions. Address, praise, and petition enact dependence in a different mode from demonstration, while technical vocabulary connects devotional practice with metaphysical commitments.',
    'Following Iamblichus, Proclus treats theurgy as a mode of unification exceeding discursive reason and grounded in divine symbols and initiative. His rigorous argument therefore culminates not in intellectual self-sufficiency but in an acknowledgment of what reasoning cannot cause by itself. Modern readers should neither hide this ritual context to make Proclus respectable nor invoke it to avoid evaluating his proofs. The tension is productive: philosophy can specify why its highest aim exceeds its own method, yet claims about privileged rites and symbols still require historical scrutiny and can concentrate authority in those recognized as competent interpreters.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'aesthetics'], ['iamblichus'], ['Hymns']),
  section('reception', 'Greek, Arabic, Latin, Byzantine, and Renaissance Proclus', [
    'Proclus’s influence traveled through selective transformations. Pseudo-Dionysius adapts Proclean causal and apophatic structures within a Christian corpus. An Arabic reworking of propositions from the Elements became the Book of Causes and circulated in Latin as an Aristotelian work until comparison with William of Moerbeke’s translation helped Thomas Aquinas identify its Proclean derivation. Byzantine readers preserved and contested Proclus; Renaissance Platonists, including Ficino, renewed direct engagement. These routes rarely carried the whole system. Translation, pseudonymous attribution, theological revision, and excerpt created several historically distinct Procluses.',
    'Later reception warns against treating “influence” as verbal resemblance alone. A procession-return pattern changes when creation, revelation, or different accounts of divine unity reorganize its premises. Conversely, doctrinal disagreement can coexist with deep dependence on Proclean questions and distinctions. The Elements traveled well because its propositions could be detached from pagan names more readily than the Platonic Theology or hymns, but that portability also distorted the original ecology of commentary and practice. Proclus became foundational partly through texts that concealed, translated, or transformed where their causal grammar came from.',
  ], ['medieval-scholasticism', 'neoplatonism', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['pseudo-dionysius', 'aquinas'], ['Elements of Theology', 'Book of Causes']),
  section('reading', 'How to read the architecture without flattening it', [
    'Begin with a limited sequence in the Elements—propositions 1–39 on unity and causation—drawing each dependency and testing the proof. Then turn to the Timaeus commentary’s introduction and a passage of close exegesis to see how philosophical distinctions function in a classroom encounter with Plato. Read selections from Platonic Theology for the method of pagan theology, and one or two hymns for the devotional voice. Marinus should be used last and critically: his ascending virtues explain how pupils wished the master’s life to embody the teaching.',
    'The guiding question is how unity can cause plurality without destroying difference. Proclus answers through participation, causal series, remaining, procession, return, and multiple mediating orders. A second question must remain beside it: how did those concepts live in teaching, reading, prayer, and a vulnerable pagan institution in Christian late antiquity? Holding both prevents the Elements from becoming a contextless diagram and prevents context from replacing argument. Proclus matters because he makes metaphysical mediation unusually explicit—and because his corpus shows that even the most formal system is sustained by pedagogical and religious practices.',
    'Reception should be approached with the same two-level method. Track a proposition or causal pattern into Pseudo-Dionysius, the Book of Causes, or a medieval commentary, but also record what new theology, language, and institutional purpose does to it. Similar vocabulary can conceal transformation; changed vocabulary can preserve an argumentative structure. This practice makes influence an inspectable historical claim rather than a line drawn between famous names, and it respects the difference between Proclus’s own pagan school world and the many traditions that found selected parts of his philosophy usable.',
  ], ['neoplatonism', 'metaphysics', 'platonism'], ['plato', 'iamblichus', 'pseudo-dionysius'], ['Elements of Theology', 'Commentary on the Timaeus', 'Platonic Theology', 'Hymns']),
];

